<h1 className="text-4xl font-bold mb-6">Why Amarcode — You shouldn't have to start over.</h1>

<div className="max-w-prose mx-auto essay-body prose-lg prose-inmaxLg">

  <p>
    Imagine you are halfway through fixing something that was supposed to take an afternoon. At first, the task seemed straightforward: a page behaved strangely after signing in, and you wanted to check whether the new configuration had taken effect. Then the sign-in prompt vanished, the environment shifted, and the problem reappeared in a different form. You closed the tab, reopened the project, and discovered that the context you had built up—what you were looking for, what you'd already tried, the precise state of the discussion—was gone. You had to start over.
  </p>

  <p>
    This experience is familiar to almost every developer. Work accumulates in fragments: a chat thread that explains a bug, a file you're editing, a configuration you're testing, a piece of code you're debugging. When you have to close the window—or when the window crashes, or when you switch machines—those fragments vanish. You lose the thread. You lose the time you invested. You start over, not from zero but from somewhere near the beginning, trying to remember what you were doing and why.
  </p>

  <p>
    Amarcode was built to answer a simple question: what if the workspace remembered everything you were doing, so you never had to start over? The idea grew out of the observation that modern AI-assisted development tools treat each session as a fresh start. You send a prompt, get a response, and the conversation resets. The history is there, but it's buried, and it doesn't carry forward the momentum of what you were building.
  </p>

  <p>
    The problem isn't just about saving chat history. It's about continuity. When you return to a project after a break, or when you switch between your laptop and your desktop, the work should pick up exactly where you left off. The conversation should resume, the files should be in the same state, and the agent should remember the context you established. That's what Amarcode provides: a persistent workspace that survives window closures, machine restarts, and even daemon restarts.
  </p>

  <p>
    Continuity isn't the only advantage. Amarcode also gives you real choice over the agent you work with. You're not locked into a single model or a single provider. If one agent isn't suited to the task, you can switch to another—one that's better at writing code, another that's better at explaining concepts, another that's better at navigating a large codebase. The agent runs on your own daemon, on your own machine, so your data never leaves your control.
  </p>

  <p>
    There's a third thread, too: the workspace itself is independent of any single window or application. Because the daemon manages the conversation, the chat history, the file state, and the agent interactions, you can close the Amarcode window, open it on another device, and your work is exactly where you left off. The window is just a view; the work happens elsewhere, and it stays waiting for you.
  </p>

  <p>
    This essay isn't a technical specification. It's an answer to the question many developers have asked, at one time or another: why does every new tool require me to start over? The answer shouldn't be "that's just how it works." It should be "we can do better."
  </p>

  <p>
    If you've ever felt that frustration—explaining your work all over again, re-creating context that already exists, starting from the beginning each time you return—this is what Amarcode solves. It's not about adding features. It's about preserving the work you've already done, so you can keep moving forward.
  </p>

</div>